<Docs edit="site/src/pages/editor.astro" title="Editor" description="The web page every Facet panel serves: build your own pages card by card on a live copy of the panel, set the built-in screens and the panel up, save to the panel." group="Use Facet" current={url('/editor/')} headings={headings}>
  <p class="lede">
    Every Facet panel serves its own editor. Open the panel's address in a browser to build your own pages, card by
    card, on a live copy of the panel, then save them to it. Nothing to install: the page comes from the panel itself.
    This is the editor's first version: more is coming, and some of it will change.
  </p>

  <figure class="page-shot"><img src={url('/editor/editor.png')} width="2560" height="1570" alt="The editor: a live copy of the panel showing its Home, and the screen's name, order and start page beside it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The editor: the live panel in the middle, what you picked beside it</figcaption></figure>

  <h2 id="open">Opening it</h2>
  <p>
    On a computer or phone on the same Wi-Fi, open <code>http://</code> and the panel's address, for example
    <code>http://192.168.1.50/</code>. The address is on the panel: a fresh panel's empty Home shows it, and so does
    <strong>Settings &gt; About</strong>. The first time after an update the editor downloads the panel's renderer
    (about 1.5 MB, a few seconds) and shows how far it has got; after that it opens at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    The panel in the middle of the page is drawn by the panel's own renderer, with the panel's own data: your lights
    and sensors, the flights overhead, the weather, your calendar. What you see there is what the glass will show.
  </p>

  <h2 id="screens">Your screens</h2>
  <p>
    The tabs along the top are your own screens, in the order you swipe through them on the panel. A fresh panel has
    one, an empty Home. <strong>+ Screen</strong> adds another (up to eight); pick a tab to name it, move it earlier or
    later, choose it as the screen the panel opens on, or remove it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cards">Cards</h2>
  <h3 id="add">Adding a card</h3>
  <p>
    Tap an empty square on the live panel (or <strong>+ Add a card at the end</strong>). Pick what it is first: a
    <strong>Screen</strong> (a card that opens another screen), <strong>Light</strong>, <strong>Switch</strong>,
    <strong>Media</strong>, <strong>Climate</strong>, <strong>Cover</strong>, <strong>Sensor</strong> or
    <strong>Other</strong>. A Sensor asks what it measures: temperature, humidity, power, energy, battery, doors and
    windows, or motion and presence. Then type part of its name or entity id; nothing is listed until you do, so a house
    with thousands of entities stays easy to search. Typing straight away, before picking a type, searches everything.
  </p>
  <div class="page-shots">
    <figure><img src={url('/editor/types.png')} width="840" height="1120" alt="Add a card: the card types, and a search box above them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The card types</figcaption></figure>
    <figure><img src={url('/editor/search.png')} width="840" height="940" alt="Searching temperature sensors: each match with its name, entity id and temperature." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A search, each match with its value</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3 id="search">Searching your entities</h3>
  <p>
    Each match shows its value the way Home Assistant does: <em>21.5 °C</em>, <em>Open</em>, <em>Detected</em>. When
    you start adding a card, the panel reads your entities from Home Assistant once (a few seconds in a large house) and
    hands the editor a compact index of them: each one's id, name, what it measures and its value. The search runs on
    that index as you type, so it is instant. The index is read again in the background when it is over a minute old.
    The panel keeps none of it once it has sent it, and only uses memory it can spare; if a very large house does not
    fit, the editor says how many entities it could not include.
  </p>
  <p>
    The search reads through the panel's own Home Assistant link (Settings &gt; Home Assistant). Without one, you can
    still add Screen cards.
  </p>

  <h3 id="sizes">Sizes and changing a card</h3>
  <p>Every card can be any size. The grid is three squares across on both panels.</p>
  <figure class="page-shot"><img src={url('/editor/sizes.png')} width="1144" height="1144" alt="A Home with a 2 by 1 Weather card, two half sensor cards, a 2 by 2 Media card and two 1 by 1 light cards." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>2 × 1, two halves, 2 × 2 and 1 × 1</figcaption></figure>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1 × 1</strong>: a tile, the icon, the name and its state.</li>
    <li><strong>Half</strong>: half a square, the name over the state. Two halves share one square: tap a half, then <strong>+ Add another half</strong> under it.</li>
    <li><strong>2 × 1</strong> and <strong>2 × 2</strong>: the card with its picture, such as the record for Media or the animated icon for Weather.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Tap a card on the live panel to change it: its size, its entity (type to search for another), its name (leave it
    empty to use the entity's own) and what a tap does (toggle it, open its screen, or nothing). Move it earlier or later
    in the grid, or remove it. Cards fill the grid in reading order; the cards after a resized one move to fit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="built-in">Built-in screens and Settings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Settings</strong> opens the panel's settings as one list, as on the panel itself:
    <strong>Screens</strong> holds the built-in screens (Clock, Weather, Media, Calendar, Energy, Overhead and the rest),
    each with whether it is on the panel, its name and its options, and the live panel shows the one you pick.
    <strong>Home Assistant</strong> sets the panel's own link (its address and a token). Then the panel's look, text
    size, units and the rest, each shown on the live panel as you change it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="save">Saving</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing reaches the panel until you press <strong>Save</strong>. It lists your changes in words, the panel checks
    them, and they go to the panel; if the panel refuses something, the editor says what and where.
    <strong>Undo</strong> puts the last save back. <strong>Back to factory</strong> starts again from one empty Home.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next">What is coming</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first version of the editor. Next: taps on the live panel that change a screen's own view (picking a
    plane on Overhead, opening a sheet) work there too, more card layouts per size, and a better 1 × 1 card for a media
    player. Follow it on <a href="https://github.com/mrdarrengriffin/facet/issues/82">GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
